sh  for some fishers the ban goes too far  we're opposed to regulatory debt  discounts it's a bad practice okay  you've already killed it you're not  wasting it before the international ban  the federal government had already  imposed one in canadian waters becoming  a champion for shark conservation we've  seen this big shift over the last like  five years or so  with makos and with other sharks and  slowly putting things in place the  international restrictions are only  temporary they could be overturned by  pressure from commercial harvesters  suggesting the makos are not in the  clear just yet  ross lord global news  ahead africa's ambitious idea what the  great green wall could mean for climate  change and migration  you're watching global national  the effects of man-made climate change  are expected to be felt most severely in  the developing world in the coming  decades droughts in africa are already  affecting a giant project that itself is  trying to hold back the effects of  mother nature the so-called great green  wall initiative involves replanting  billions of hectares across the width of  the continent but as redmond shannon  reports the long-term consequences of  the plan are still unknown  thousands of years ago what is now the  sahara desert was green and lush it  dried out as part of a natural cycle of  climate change in recent decades  concerns about ongoing deforestation and  desertification led to a plan of action  the great green wall was launched in  2007. the initial idea was to build an 8  000 kilometer long forest from one side  of africa to the other it would be 15  kilometers wide and be used as a buffer  against deforestation  in 2012 the focus shifted from trees to  human development the wall became a set  of sustainable land use projects across  the belt so far it's thought about 15 of  the project has been completed however  drought and maintenance have been issues  this man in mauritania says he's barely  seen any rain in three years so  solar-powered pumps are being used to  irrigate palm trees barriers shield them  from the wind so they can shield  villages from encroaching sands  the country's environment minister says  it shows how the pan-african project is  about more than just planting trees it's  about improving the lives and skills of  those involved  this initiative in senegal looks to do  the same special circular gardens  designed to better resist drought  if successful the great green wall could  also help stem the tide of migration  from many parts of africa  any type of geoengineering project  because at the end of the day this is a  sort of a geoengineering project  they have to be validated with  scientific studies in montreal francesco  palzada is researching what the broader  consequences of planting so many trees  in these areas would actually be on  regional climates you know you think i  mean i'm just planting trees what is  going to happen by planting trees  but actually the vegetation has a very  strong feedback he says his ongoing  research suggests that could include  more rain but also more extreme  temperatures high and low as well as  more intense monsoons further afield  project coordinators say billions of  dollars more are needed to see the  mammoth project completed this decade  even though its long-term consequences  are still unknown redmond shannon global  news london  this is right off the wall as far as  community service goes next the bc man  using a transit bus to deliver kindness  to strangers  [Music]  a big wallop of winter isn't exactly the  norm on vancouver island so when it does  happen it can be difficult to deal with  in one seaside town a group of good  samaritans put their shovels musical  talents and a borrowed bc transit bus to  work to help clear out all the recent  snow kylie stanton reports  okay to travel  thank you jd ludlow may drive this bus  for a living  but what is it that drives him  good question i wake up every day  thinking ho